IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `wk-nouns`
IMPORT `wk-agreements`

-- Antitrust Guidelines for Business Activities Affecting Workers (2025), section 6
-- (independent contractors) and section 7 (false earnings claims). Section 8 (reporting
-- violations) states no rule and is not encoded (NOTES.md, coverage).
--
-- AGENCY ENFORCEMENT POLICY, NOT LAW (n.1 [pdf page 2]). Each result is a sentence of the
-- text with its own modal. EMPTY and NOTHING as in wk-agreements.l4 (fork F23).

§ `Sections 6 and 7: independent contractors, and false earnings claims`

§§ `Section 6: independent contractors`

DECLARE `What section 6 says of the agreement` IS ONE OF
    `the Overview: an agreement between businesses to fix the compensation that each pays to independent contractors may violate the antitrust laws, just as an agreement between businesses to fix the wages each pays to workers may violate the antitrust laws`
    `an agreement between two or more competing platforms to fix the compensation of independent contractors offering their services via the platforms may constitute the type of per se violation of the antitrust laws that exposes the platforms to criminal liability`

-- 6 [pdf page 11] and the Overview's account of it [pdf page 3]:
--   * "The antitrust laws prohibit anticompetitive conduct directed at workers, which
--     includes both employees and independent contractors." Given effect in sections 1
--     and 2, which do not read the kind of workers (fork F10); here, the two sentences
--     that speak of independent contractors.
--   * The Overview: "an agreement between businesses to fix the compensation that each
--     pays to independent contractors may violate the antitrust laws, just as" one to fix
--     wages may. No condition on competition is stated, and none is read.
--   * "an agreement between two or more competing platforms to fix the compensation of
--     independent contractors offering their services via the platforms may constitute
--     the type of per se violation of the antitrust laws that the exposes the platforms to
--     criminal liability" [sic]; the result's name drops the stray word (NOTES.md, section
--     11) (fork F11).
-- "Fix the compensation" is section 1's wage-fixing substance. For the starting point,
-- which section 1 says only "may be a form of wage fixing" (1 [pdf page 6]), whether either
-- sentence is said turns on what the text does not say, so the answer is NOTHING (forks
-- F6, F23). The description of independent contractors ("typically are hired to perform
-- discrete jobs", n.44) is not a test: whether the workers are independent contractors
-- is the caller's. Guards (NOTES.md, section 12): NOTHING when whether there is an
-- agreement has not been determined; when the two are the same business; for the
-- starting point; and for platforms fixing independent contractors' compensation when
-- whether they compete for workers is not stated, or they are only potentially competing
-- (`6: whether the text says the agreement is one between competing platforms to fix the
-- compensation of independent contractors`). EMPTY when no agreement is found or no
-- sentence applies.
@ref 6 [pdf page 11]; Overview [pdf page 3]
GIVEN x IS A `Agreement between businesses relating to workers`
GIVETH A MAYBE (LIST OF `What section 6 says of the agreement`)
`6: what section 6 says of the agreement` MEANS
    CONSIDER x's `the finding on whether there is an agreement`
    WHEN `the agencies identify an agreement between the businesses` THEN
        BRANCH
          IF `General principles: the two businesses are the same business` (x's `the businesses`) THEN NOTHING
          IF (NOT contractors) THEN JUST EMPTY
          IF startingPoint THEN NOTHING
          IF (NOT fixing) THEN JUST EMPTY
          IF platforms EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
          OTHERWISE JUST (catMaybes (LIST (JUST `the Overview: an agreement between businesses to fix the compensation that each pays to independent contractors may violate the antitrust laws, just as an agreement between businesses to fix the wages each pays to workers may violate the antitrust laws`),
                                          (IF platforms EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST `an agreement between two or more competing platforms to fix the compensation of independent contractors offering their services via the platforms may constitute the type of per se violation of the antitrust laws that exposes the platforms to criminal liability` ELSE NOTHING)))
    WHEN `the agencies find no agreement between the businesses` THEN JUST EMPTY
    WHEN `whether there is an agreement has not been determined` THEN NOTHING
  WHERE
    contractors MEANS NOT (x's `the workers concerned` EQUALS `employees`)
    fixing MEANS `1: what the agreement is, in substance` (x's `what the businesses agree`) EQUALS JUST `a wage-fixing agreement`
    startingPoint MEANS `1: what the agreement is, in substance` (x's `what the businesses agree`) EQUALS JUST `an agreement that may be a form of wage fixing`
    platforms MEANS `6: whether the text says the agreement is one between competing platforms to fix the compensation of independent contractors` x

§§ `Section 7: false earnings claims`

DECLARE `What section 7 says of the claim` IS ONE OF
    `the Overview: false claims about workers' potential earnings may violate federal laws against unfair, deceptive, or abusive practices`
    `the agencies may investigate and take action against businesses that make false or misleading claims about potential earnings that workers, including both employees and independent contractors, may realize`

-- 7 [pdf page 12]: "The Agencies may investigate and take action against businesses that
-- make false or misleading claims about potential earnings that workers (including both
-- employees and independent contractors) may realize." The Overview [pdf page 3]: "false
-- claims about workers' potential earnings may violate federal laws against unfair,
-- deceptive, or abusive practices": said of false claims, not of misleading ones (fork
-- F19). Listed in the text's order, the Overview's [pdf page 3] before the body's [pdf page
-- 12], as section 6 lists its own. The kind of workers is not read. The FTC's actions
-- (n.45-n.48) are fixtures.
@ref 7 [pdf page 12]; Overview [pdf page 3]
GIVEN c IS A `Claim about workers' potential earnings`
GIVETH A LIST OF `What section 7 says of the claim`
`7: what section 7 says of the claim` MEANS
    catMaybes (LIST (IF c's `the claim is false` THEN JUST `the Overview: false claims about workers' potential earnings may violate federal laws against unfair, deceptive, or abusive practices` ELSE NOTHING),
                    (IF c's `the claim is false` OR c's `the claim is misleading` THEN JUST `the agencies may investigate and take action against businesses that make false or misleading claims about potential earnings that workers, including both employees and independent contractors, may realize` ELSE NOTHING))
